    53	\begin{document}
    54	
    55	\begin{abstract}
    56	We give a deterministic algorithm that receives only an integer $N$ and outputs its complete
    57	prime factorisation. On every input its cost is that of the Harvey--Hittmeir algorithm,
    58	$O(N^{1/5}\log^{16/5}N/(\log\log N)^{3/5})$ bit operations, up to lower-order terms. Averaged
    59	over the uniform distribution on the $n$-bit semiprimes $N=pq$ with primes $p<q\le2p$, its
    60	cost is $N^{1/6}(\log N)^{O(1)}$. We know of no previously proved average-case or almost-all
    61	deterministic exponent below $1/5$ for this population.
    62	
    63	The engine is a deterministic weighted collision search, which is a Lehman-type method. It
    64	takes the McKee--Pinch divisibility event $\ell\mid Bp-Aq$ over all primes $\ell\equiv3\pmod
    65	4$ in four dyadic bands near $N^{1/6}$ and all small coprime weights $(A,B)$. It adds the
    66	Harvey--Hittmeir modulus-squared lock, and one baby-step list per band, shared by all primes
    67	of the band through cofactor-powered handles. One run with weight height $H$ costs
    68	$N^{1/6}H^{5/3}(\log N)^{O(1)}$ on every input and never outputs a wrong answer. A run fails
    69	on at most an $O((\log N)/H^2)$ and an $O((\log N)^4/H^4)$ fraction of the pairs. The second
    70	bound comes from a two-variable fourth-moment estimate. It gives, for every
    71	$\varepsilon\le1/40$, cost $N^{1/6+5\varepsilon/12+o(1)}$ with exceptional fraction
    72	$O(N^{-\varepsilon})$. The complete algorithm reruns the search with heights
    73	$H\leftarrow H^{6/5}$ behind a baby-step giant-step detector for primes with small
    74	$\ord_q(2)$. This works because the proved failure fraction decays faster in $H$ than the cost
    75	grows.
    76	
    77	We prove two further results. First, the worst case reduces to coverage alone: a modified
    78	search factors every balanced $N$ whose true pair is caught by one band. So a pair-covering
    79	hypothesis implies worst-case cost $N^{1/6+o(1)}$, and any covering theorem with weight
    80	height below $Y^{1/2}J^{-3/8}$ would beat $1/5$ in the worst case. Second, in a sumset
    81	collision-search cost model with residue-local, degree-one targets, reaching the true factor
    82	of a constant fraction of the pairs requires cost $N^{1/6}/(\log N)^{O(1)}$. Our algorithm
    83	attains this up to polylogarithmic factors. The result says nothing about other kinds of
    84	algorithm, and at practical sizes Harvey--Hittmeir is faster.
    85	\end{abstract}
