   100	%======================================================================
   101	\section{Introduction and statement of results}\label{sec:intro}
   102	%======================================================================
   103	
   104	\subsection{Background}
   105	Few exponents are known for deterministic integer factoring with rigorous bounds.
   106	\begin{itemize}
   107	\item Trial division costs $N^{1/2}$.
   108	\item Lehman's method \cite{lehman1974} gives $N^{1/3+o(1)}$.
   109	\item The Pollard--Strassen method \cite{pollard1974,strassen1976} gives $N^{1/4+o(1)}$. It was
   110	  refined by Bostan--Gaudry--Schost \cite{bgs2007} and Costa--Harvey \cite{costaharvey}.
   111	\item Hittmeir saved a subexponential factor at exponent $1/4$ \cite{hittmeir2018}. He then
   112	  proved exponent $2/9$ with a time--space trade-off for Lehman's method \cite{hittmeir2021}.
   113	\item Harvey \cite{harvey2021} proved exponent $1/5$. Harvey and Hittmeir \cite{hh2022}
   114	  improved the logarithmic factor, and Gao, Feng, Hu and Pan \cite{gao2025} improved it again
   115	  for balanced semiprimes, at the same exponent.
   116	\end{itemize}
   117	Below $1/5$ the known routes are conditional or partial.
   118	\begin{itemize}
   119	\item Umans and Wang \cite{umanswang2025} showed that a divisor-cover conjecture would give
   120	  $1/6$. He and Sahai \cite{hesahai2026} refuted that conjecture's one-dimensional
   121	  arithmetic-progression form.
   122	\item Pollard's $p-1$ method succeeds on a positive proportion of nearly balanced semiprimes at
   123	  exponents approaching $1/(4\sqrt e)\approx0.1516$, by Friedlander's theorem on shifted primes
   124	  \cite{friedlander1989}. Ward--Maestro \cite[Prop.~35.3]{wardmaestro2026} record this, and
   125	  note that it gives no average-time bound for a complete algorithm. The failure set has
   126	  positive density.
   127	\item Carella \cite{carella} claims a deterministic $O(N^{1/6+\varepsilon})$ algorithm. Its
   128	  displayed proof has a normalisation defect (Section~\ref{sec:prior}).
   129	\end{itemize}
   130	
   131	This paper is about the \emph{average} cost over the hardest natural population, the
   132	semiprimes with two factors of comparable size. For unrestricted semiprimes the average case is
   133	easy: almost all have a factor below $N^{\delta}$ \cite{crisanerban}. Balanced semiprimes are
   134	the opposite. Every method above is analysed in the worst case or on a positive proportion,
   135	and we are not aware of a proved average-case or almost-all deterministic exponent below $1/5$
   136	for them.
   137	
   138	\subsection{Population and cost}
   139	Let $n$ be the bit length, $X=2^n$, $P=2^{n/2}$, and
   140	\[
   141	  \Sn=\{(p,q): p<q\le 2p,\ p,q\ \text{prime},\ 2^{n-1}\le pq<2^n\}.
   142	\]
   143	We count $\Sn$ by pairs. By unique factorisation this is the same as counting the semiprimes
   144	$N=pq$, and ``uniform on $\Sn$'' means uniform on these semiprimes. For $(p,q)\in\Sn$ we have
   145	$p\in[\sqrt{N/2},\sqrt N]\subset[P/2,P]$ and $q\in(P/\sqrt2,\sqrt2P)$. The prime number theorem
   146	gives $c_0P^2/n^2\le|\Sn|$ for an absolute $c_0>0$ and all large $n$. Costs are bit operations
   147	on a multitape Turing machine, with integer multiplication in time $M(b)=O(b\log b)$
   148	\cite{hvdh2021}. $\HH(N)=O(N^{1/5}\log^{16/5}N/(\log\log N)^{3/5})$ denotes the Harvey--Hittmeir
   149	bound \cite{hh2022}.
   150	
   151	\subsection{Main results}
   152	
   153	\begin{theorem}[average-case complete factoring; proved]\label{thm:AV}
   154	There is a deterministic algorithm $\LAD$ and an absolute constant $c$ with the following
   155	properties.
   156	\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
   157	\item \emph{Completeness.} On every input $N\ge2$, $\LAD$ outputs the complete prime
   158	  factorisation of $N$.
   159	\item \emph{Worst case.} On every $n$-bit input its cost is at most
   160	  \[\HH(N)+O(n^{c+4}2^{7n/36})+O(n^{O(1)}2^{3n/20})=O(\HH(N)).\]
   161	\item \emph{Average over $\Sn$.} For all large $n$,
   162	  \[
   163	    \frac1{|\Sn|}\sum_{(p,q)\in\Sn}\mathrm{cost}(pq)=O\bigl(N^{1/6}(\log N)^{c+1}\log\log N\bigr).
   164	  \]
   165	\item \emph{Other distributions.} The bound in (iii), multiplied by $K$, holds for every
   166	  distribution on $\Sn$ with point masses at most $K/|\Sn|$.
   167	\end{enumerate}
   168	\end{theorem}
   169	
   170	The constant $c$ is the polylogarithmic exponent in the cost of one run of the search
   171	(Proposition~\ref{prop:onerun}(ii)). Item (iv) covers, for instance, the product of two
   172	independent uniform $n/2$-bit primes conditioned to be distinct, for even $n$: its support lies
   173	in $\mathcal S_n\cup\mathcal S_{n-1}$ and its mass ratio is bounded (apply (iii) to each shell).
   174	It says nothing about uniform $n$-bit integers, or about key-generation rules that restrict $p$
   175	and $q$ further.
   176	
   177	\begin{theorem}[almost-all factoring at fixed cost; proved]\label{thm:D}
   178	There are deterministic algorithms $\DCL_{n,H}$ (Section~\ref{sec:alg}) with the following
   179	property. Each sees only $N$. Each outputs either a proper divisor of $N$ or \texttt{FAIL}, and
   180	never anything else.
   181	\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
   182	\item For every fixed $\varepsilon\in(0,1/40]$ and $n\ge n_0(\varepsilon)$, the choice
   183	  $H=\lceil n2^{\varepsilon n/4}\rceil$ gives cost $N^{1/6+5\varepsilon/12}(\log N)^{O(1)}$ on
   184	  every $n$-bit input. It outputs \texttt{FAIL} on $O(N^{-\varepsilon})|\Sn|$ pairs of $\Sn$.
   185	\item For every fixed $a>0$, the choice $H=\lceil n^{1+a}\rceil$ gives cost
   186	  $N^{1/6}(\log N)^{O(1+a)}$. It outputs \texttt{FAIL} on
   187	  $O\bigl((\log N)^{-\max(4a,\,1+2a)}\bigr)|\Sn|$ pairs.
   188	\end{enumerate}
   189	\end{theorem}
   190	
   191	An earlier version of (a), proved with the arithmetic large sieve alone, gave cost
   192	$N^{1/6+5\varepsilon/6+o(1)}$ for $\varepsilon\le1/60$ (Theorem~D$'$ of the project notes;
   193	Remark~\ref{rem:Dprime}). The improvement comes from the two-variable fourth moment of
   194	Section~\ref{sec:fourth}.
   195	
   196	\begin{theorem}[worst case from coverage; proved]\label{thm:R}
   197	There is a deterministic algorithm $\DCLwc$ (Section~\ref{sec:worst}) that takes as public
   198	parameters a weight set and blocks of primes. Suppose $N=pq\in\Sn$ and some block contains a
   199	prime $\ell$ with $\ell\mid Bp-Aq$ for some weight $(A,B)$. Then $\DCLwc$ outputs a proper divisor
   200	of $N$. Its cost is $N^{1/6+o(1)}$ plus the cost of the shared collision search over the blocks.
   201	No hypothesis on any multiplicative order is needed.
   202	\end{theorem}
   203	
   204	\begin{corollary}[proved]\label{cor:conditional}
   205	\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
   206	\item If Hypothesis~\ref{hyp:DCHpair} ($\mathrm{DCH}_{\mathrm{pair}}(\kappa)$) holds for some
   207	  $\kappa$, then every $N\in\Sn$ is factored deterministically in $N^{1/6+o(1)}$ bit operations.
   208	\item Let $Y$ be the band scale and $J\asymp Y/\log Y$ the number of band primes. Suppose every
   209	  pair of $\Sn$ has a band prime dividing some $Bp-Aq$ with weight height $H\le Y^{1/2}J^{-c}$,
   210	  for some $c>3/8$. Then the deterministic worst-case exponent on $\Sn$ is at most
   211	  $(1-c)/(7/2-c)<1/5$.
   212	\end{enumerate}
   213	\end{corollary}
   214	
   215	\begin{theorem}[true-factor floor for local designs; proved in the model]\label{thm:T1}
   216	Consider a sumset collision search in which labels are charged, at most $X$ targets are issued
   217	and the windows have total size at most $X^2$. Suppose its targets are residue-local and of
   218	degree one (Section~\ref{sec:lower}). Then it reaches the true factor of at most
   219	$Cn^2X^{3/2}N^{-1/4}|\Sn|$ pairs. Success on a constant fraction $\sigma$ of $\Sn$ therefore needs
   220	$X\ge c\,\sigma^{2/3}N^{1/6}(\log N)^{-4/3}$.
   221	\end{theorem}
   222	
   223	Theorem~\ref{thm:D}(b) attains the bound of Theorem~\ref{thm:T1} up to polylogarithmic factors:
   224	the algorithm $\DCL_{n,H}$ is dominated, at constant-factor cost, by a design of this kind
   225	(Proposition~\ref{prop:Dinmodel}). This replaces a claim made in an earlier draft that the
   226	algorithm is ``optimal in its model''. That claim was based on a lower bound for covering
   227	\emph{every} integer candidate (Appendix~\ref{app:full}), which does not bound an algorithm that
   228	only needs to reach the true factor.
   229	
   230	\begin{remark}[scope]\label{rem:scope}
   231	Each item below is easy to over-read, so we state them plainly.
   232	\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
   233	\item Theorems~\ref{thm:AV} and~\ref{thm:D} concern inputs drawn from $\Sn$, with exceptions
   234	  counted by pairs. Neither gives a worst-case bound below $1/5$. Theorem~\ref{thm:AV} is an
   235	  \emph{expected-time} statement for a complete algorithm; it is not a high-probability
   236	  statement beyond what Theorem~\ref{thm:D} gives.
   237	\item Only ratios $q/p\le2$ are treated. The ratio-band extension is discussed in
   238	  Section~\ref{sec:unbalanced}.
   239	\item ``Never wrong'' means that every output is validated.
   240	\item Theorem~\ref{thm:T1} is a theorem inside a stated cost model. It is not a lower bound for
   241	  factoring. Pollard's $p-1$ method, which lies outside the model, already reaches a positive
   242	  proportion below $N^{1/6}$ (\cite[Prop.~35.3]{wardmaestro2026}, \cite{friedlander1989}).
   243	\item All statements are asymptotic. At 40--80 bits the search is $2.3$--$4.9$ times more
   244	  expensive than Harvey--Hittmeir in our cost proxy (Section~\ref{sec:experiments}).
   245	\end{enumerate}
   246	\end{remark}
   247	
   248	\subsection{How it works}
   249	Fix a prime $\ell$ and coprime weights $(A,B)$.
   250	\begin{itemize}
   251	\item If $\ell\mid Bp-Aq$, then $p\bmod\ell$ is one of the two square roots of $AN/B$ modulo
   252	  $\ell$, and these roots are computable from $N$.
   253	\item On that residue class the weighted sum $Bp+Aq$ is determined modulo $\ell^2$ (the
   254	  Harvey--Hittmeir lock). So $g=(Bp+Aq-c)/\ell^2$ is an integer in a window of length about
   255	  $H\sqrt N/\ell^2$.
   256	\item Exponentiating, $2^{(L/\ell^2)(B+AN-c)}\equiv\gamma^{g}\pmod p$, with $\gamma=2^{L}$ and
   257	  $L=\prod\ell^2$ over the band. So every target, for every prime of the band, is one giant step
   258	  against the band's single baby list $\gamma^0,\dots,\gamma^{m-1}$.
   259	\item Primes near $N^{1/6}$ balance the baby list ($\approx H\sqrt N/\ell^2$) against the number
   260	  of targets ($\approx H^2\ell$).
   261	\item Coverage of the true $p$ is a sieve problem: the large sieve gives the failure fraction
   262	  $O(n/H^2)$, and a fourth moment over both variables gives $O(n^4/H^4)$.
   263	\item The collision modulo $p$ is an exact identity, so no order lemma is needed there. It
   264	  remains to rule out a simultaneous collision modulo $q$. Four disjoint bands make one band
   265	  coprime to $q-1$, and on that band a two-sided collision forces $\ord_q(2)$ to divide a
   266	  specific small expression, which is rare.
   267	\end{itemize}
   268	The failure fraction falls like $H^{-2}$ while the cost grows like $H^{5/3}$. So rerunning on
   269	failure with $H\leftarrow H^{6/5}$ costs $N^{1/6}\polylog$ per rung on average, and after
   270	$O(\log\log N)$ rungs only a fraction $N^{-1/30}$ of pairs remains for the $N^{1/5}$ fallback.
   271	The order condition is handled up front by a baby-step giant-step search for the least $k$ with
   272	$\gcd(2^k-1,N)>1$, up to $N^{3/10}$.
   273	
   274	\subsection{What is new, and what is not}
   275	We claim novelty only in the form ``we are not aware of''. Each ingredient is known
   276	(Section~\ref{sec:prior}).
   277	\begin{itemize}
   278	\item The divisibility event $\lambda p\equiv\pm\mu q\pmod k$ with small multipliers, the
   279	  $N$-determined residues and a modulus-squared reduction are due to McKee and Pinch
   280	  \cite[\S5]{mckeepinch1996}. So is a \emph{heuristic} balanced method over primes
   281	  $k\approx N^{1/4}$.
   282	\item The modulus-squared lock in the form we use is Harvey--Hittmeir
   283	  \cite[Prop.~3.3]{hh2022}.
   284	\item Shared baby-step/giant-step collision search with multipoint evaluation over $\Z/N\Z$ is
   285	  Harvey's \cite[Alg.~4.1]{harvey2021}. Exact-match handling is \cite[Alg.~4.2]{harvey2021}.
   286	\item The order-selection tools behind Theorem~\ref{thm:R} adapt Harvey--Hittmeir 2026
   287	  \cite{hh2026}.
   288	\item The large sieve is Montgomery's \cite{montgomery1968,mv1973}.
   289	\end{itemize}
   290	We are not aware of a prior statement of any of the following:
   291	\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
   292	\item a band of primes at $N^{1/6}H^{-1/3}$, made possible by one baby list per band shared
   293	  through cofactor-powered handles;
   294	\item a proof that the event catches the true factor for all but $O(n/H^2)$, or
   295	  $O(n^4/H^4)$, of the pairs;
   296	\item one-sided extraction without an order hypothesis (the clean-band argument);
   297	\item the observation that the proved tail beats the cost growth, which turns an almost-all
   298	  algorithm into a complete one with average exponent $1/6$;
   299	\item the reduction of the worst case to coverage (Theorem~\ref{thm:R});
   300	\item a true-factor lower bound for local designs (Theorem~\ref{thm:T1}).
   301	\end{enumerate}
   302	The full-candidate covering floor $N^{1/6}$ of Appendix~\ref{app:full} is \emph{not} new. The
   303	same exponent, by a closely related argument, is in Ward--Maestro \cite[Thm.~3.6]{wardmaestro2026}
   304	(2 September 2026) for Lehman forms without class locks. Our appendix version also allows
   305	arbitrary lock moduli, and we derived it independently and later.
   306	
   307	\subsection{Organisation}
   308	\begin{itemize}
   309	\item Section~\ref{sec:prior} reviews prior art.
   310	\item Section~\ref{sec:alg} defines the search $\DCL_{n,H}$, and Section~\ref{sec:onerun}
   311	  analyses one run.
   312	\item Section~\ref{sec:fourth} proves the fourth-moment coverage bound and
   313	  Theorem~\ref{thm:D}.
   314	\item Section~\ref{sec:AV} proves Theorem~\ref{thm:AV}.
   315	\item Section~\ref{sec:worst} treats the worst case (Theorem~\ref{thm:R},
   316	  Corollary~\ref{cor:conditional}).
   317	\item Section~\ref{sec:lower} proves Theorem~\ref{thm:T1}.
   318	\item Section~\ref{sec:unbalanced} is a placeholder on unbalanced ratios.
   319	\item Section~\ref{sec:experiments} reports pre-registered experiments, and
   320	  Section~\ref{sec:open} lists open problems.
   321	\item Appendix~\ref{app:full} contains the full-candidate covering bounds.
   322	\end{itemize}
