   338	\paragraph{McKee--Pinch 1996, \S5: the direct precursor.}
   339	We read \S5 (pp.~221--223) of \cite{mckeepinch1996} from the public scan indexed at
   340	\url{https://cr.yp.to/bib/1996/mckee-deterministic.html}. It contains five things.
   341	\begin{itemize}
   342	\item Lehmer's speed-up: if $p\equiv q\equiv1\pmod k$, Fermat's method runs in $O(q/k^2)$.
   343	\item An extension: for $p\equiv\pm q\pmod k$, Fermat runs in $O(q/k^{2-\epsilon})$, with $u$
   344	  or $v$ in $n=u^2-v^2$ determined modulo $k^2$ from square roots modulo $k$. This is a
   345	  modulus-squared lock.
   346	\item Multipliers: if $\lambda p\equiv\pm\mu q\pmod k$, the same method splits $\lambda\mu n$.
   347	\item A deterministic $O(n^{1/3+\epsilon})$ algorithm, built on a pigeonhole lemma.
   348	\item A heuristic balanced method, described by the authors as ``not however an algorithm''.
   349	  It takes primes $k\in(n^{1/4},2n^{1/4})$ and small $\lambda,\mu$, and hopes that
   350	  $\lambda p-\mu q\equiv0\pmod k$ for some of them, at expected cost $O(n^{1/4+\epsilon})$.
   351	\end{itemize}
   352	So the core event of this paper is theirs: a band prime divides a small-weight combination,
   353	with $N$-determined residues and a modulus-squared reduction. We trade a larger window for
   354	fewer, smaller primes through one baby list per band. We also prove the coverage statement, the
   355	extraction, the average-time theorem and the worst-case reduction.
   356	
   357	\paragraph{Hittmeir 2018, 2021.}
   358	Hittmeir \cite{hittmeir2018} combines baby-step/giant-step with sieving of $p+q$ modulo many
   359	small primes and proves $N^{1/4}\exp(-\Omega(\log N/\log\log N))$ [sweep].
   360	Hittmeir~\cite{hittmeir2021} proves $N^{2/9+o(1)}$ through a time--space trade-off for Lehman's
   361	method [sweep]. Hyperbola sieving over many small primes is therefore old. What differs here is
   362	that each prime is used \emph{selectively}: only the at most two classes reached by a short weight
   363	vector are used, rather than all classes being covered.
   364	
   365	\paragraph{Harvey 2021; Harvey--Hittmeir 2022.}
   366	Harvey \cite{harvey2021} proves the deterministic bound $O(N^{1/5}\log^{16/5}N)$, using a single
   367	shared baby-step giant-step search over Lehman's family. He uses the congruences
   368	\[\alpha^{aq+bp}\equiv\alpha^{aN+b}\pmod p\]
   369	and multipoint evaluation over $\Z/N\Z$
   370	(Alg.~4.1), with exact matches handled in Alg.~4.2 [sweep]. Harvey--Hittmeir \cite{hh2022} save a
   371	factor $(\log\log N)^{3/5}$. Their Proposition~3.3 contains the congruence $aq-bp\equiv0\pmod m$
   372	and the modulus-squared lock we use as Lemma~\ref{lem:lock}, with a single modulus and
   373	lattice-chosen weights [sweep]. Harvey remarks that exponent $1/6$ would need a ``fully
   374	square-root speedup'' of Lehman's search [sweep].
   375	
   376	\paragraph{Harvey--Hittmeir 2026; Oznovich--Volk 2025.}
   377	These works give deterministic algorithms that find an element of large multiplicative order
   378	modulo $N$, or a factor \cite{hh2026,oznovichvolk2025} [sweep]. Theorem~\ref{thm:R} adapts
   379	Harvey--Hittmeir's smooth-number subgroup argument and their order dichotomy
   380	(\cite[Lemmas~2.1, 2.3, 2.4 and \S3.1]{hh2026} [sweep]) to a one-sided reading: the first $r$
   381	with $\gcd(b^r-1,N)>1$ gives a factor unless the two orders coincide. Large order modulo $N$ does
   382	not give large order modulo a specific prime. Our average-case theorems therefore avoid order
   383	questions altogether.
   384	
   385	\paragraph{Ward--Maestro 2026.}
   386	We read the report \cite{wardmaestro2026} (dated 2 September 2026, 110 pages, six parts). Three
   387	parts of it bear on this paper.
   388	\begin{itemize}
   389	\item \emph{The covering floors.} The report defines an ``oblivious Lehman-cell BSGS''
   390	  model (Definition~2.1): forms $aq+bp$ with $a,b\ge1$ and tested windows that must cover every
   391	  real $p$ in the range. In that model it proves floors of $N^{1/3}$ candidates
   392	  (Corollary~3.3), $N^{1/5}$ with materialised giants (Theorem~3.4) and $N^{1/6}$ with free
   393	  giants (Theorem~3.6). It also treats unbalanced ranges, forms of bounded degree
   394	  (Theorem~9.4), thinning by residue classes modulo $q\le N^{1/20}$ (Theorem~2.8), and
   395	  randomised covering (Remark~3.5).
   396	\item \emph{Partial information.} It gives deterministic searches given partial information,
   397	  including $N^{1/5+o(1)}M^{-2/5}$ given $p\bmod M$ (Proposition~14.2).
   398	\item \emph{The $p-1$ result.} It proves the positive-proportion $p-1$ result quoted in the
   399	  introduction (Proposition~35.3).
   400	\end{itemize}
   401	Its $N^{1/6}$ floor and the main idea of its proof (the curvature bound on the covering sum,
   402	Lemma~3.2) pre-date and overlap our Appendix~\ref{app:full}. Our version allows arbitrary lock
   403	moduli and charges window counts per modulus, but the exponent and its proof idea are theirs.
   404	\begin{itemize}
   405	\item The report contains no algorithm with an almost-all or average-case guarantee below
   406	  $N^{1/5}$.
   407	\item It does not use many-prime selective covering, a prime band near $N^{1/6}$, or a baby list
   408	  shared across moduli.
   409	\item It considers no true-factor counting under residue-local keys of the kind in
   410	  Theorem~\ref{thm:T1}.
   411	\end{itemize}
   412	Proposition~14.2 there is related in spirit to our targets, which compute $p\bmod\ell$ from the
   413	event $\ell\mid Bp-Aq$ rather than receive it. Ward--Maestro cite Carella's claim as unverified.
   414	
   415	\paragraph{St\u{a}nic\u{a}--Mulder--Hittmeir 2026.}
   416	\cite{smh2026} studies unions of weighted modular-hyperbola sieve sets, their CRT behaviour and
   417	elliptic-trace obstructions [sweep]. Its averages are over finite-field parameters. The sweep
   418	found no global coverage theorem over prime pairs in it.
   419	
   420	\paragraph{Gao--Feng--Hu--Pan 2025.}
   421	For balanced semiprimes, \cite{gao2025} claims a deterministic algorithm of cost
   422	\[O(N^{1/5}\log^{13/5}N/(\log\log N)^{3/5})\]
   423	via rank-3 lattices [sweep]. The exponent
   424	remains $1/5$.
   425	
   426	\paragraph{Umans--Wang 2025; He--Sahai 2026.}
   427	Umans and Wang \cite{umanswang2025} show that a Strong Prefactored Divisor Conjecture would
   428	give deterministic exponent $1/6$ (their Thms.~5.2 and~5.5). He and Sahai \cite{hesahai2026}
   429	refute the one-dimensional arithmetic-progression version at the proposed parameters; higher
   430	ranks remain open [sweep]. Our results are unconditional but average-case. The covering
   431	statement that would make our method worst-case (Hypothesis~\ref{hyp:DCHpair}) is a different
   432	object. We know of no implication between it and the Umans--Wang conjecture in either
   433	direction, and we know of no transfer of the He--Sahai argument to it.
   434	
   435	\paragraph{Carella, arXiv:1308.2891.}
   436	Carella \cite{carella} states (Theorem~1.1) a deterministic algorithm that factors a balanced
   437	$N=pq$ in $O(N^{1/6+\varepsilon})$ operations. The algorithm guesses the least significant
   438	$(\log N)/6$ bits of $p$ and recovers the rest by a bivariate small-root method. We read its \S5.
   439	The argument there runs as follows.
   440	\begin{itemize}
   441	\item Put $B=\lfloor N^{1/6}\rfloor$. Write $p=x_1B^3+Bx+x_0$ and $q=y_1B^3+By+y_0$ with
   442	  $|x|,|y|\le N^{1/3}$, where $y_0\equiv Nx_0^{-1}\pmod B$.
   443	\item Equation~(17) considers $f(x,y)=pq-N$ and lists its coefficients.
   444	\item The listed coefficients of $x$ and $y$ are $y_1B^3+y_0$ and $x_1B^3+x_0$, and the
   445	  coefficient of $xy$ is $B^2$.
   446	\item From this list $f$ is declared primitive. With $X=Y=N^{1/3}$ its height is $W=N$, and the
   447	  small-root condition $XY<W^{2/3}$ is invoked.
   448	\end{itemize}
   449	Expanding the product directly gives linear coefficients $B(y_1B^3+y_0)$ and $B(x_1B^3+x_0)$.
   450	The constant term $(x_1B^3+x_0)(y_1B^3+y_0)-N$ is also divisible by $B$, because
   451	$x_0y_0\equiv N\pmod B$. So $f$ has content divisible by $B$. The small-root theorem applies to
   452	the primitive polynomial $f/B$, whose height is about $N/B=N^{5/6}$. For it, $XY=N^{2/3}$ exceeds
   453	$(N^{5/6})^{2/3}=N^{5/9}$, so the condition fails. (Even before this correction the condition
   454	holds only with equality.) This matches the familiar fact that small-root methods need about
   455	$(\log N)/4$ known bits of a factor, not $(\log N)/6$. We conclude only that the displayed
   456	argument does not establish the claimed bound. The same defect was found independently in three
   457	of our AI reviews.
   458	
   459	\paragraph{The large sieve; moments.}
   460	We use the arithmetic large sieve in Montgomery's form for squarefree moduli
   461	\cite{montgomery1968}, with the Montgomery--Vaughan constant \cite{mv1973}. The barrier remark
   462	in Section~\ref{sec:fourth} uses Peled--Yadin--Yehudayoff \cite{pyy2011}.
   463	
   464	\paragraph{Summary.}
   465	Our own searches, and GPT-6.1-sol sweeps checking about twenty items, found no unconditional,
   466	oracle-free deterministic result below exponent $1/5$ that factors almost all, or on average,
   467	balanced prime pairs. The only exception is Carella's claim above. This is the outcome of a
   468	search, not a proof of priority.
   469	
   470	%======================================================================
   471	\section{The search \texorpdfstring{$\DCL_{n,H}$}{DCL}}\label{sec:alg}
   472	%======================================================================
   473	
   474	\subsection{Parameters}
   475	All parameters depend on $n$ and on an integer weight height $H$, never on $N$. We assume
   476	throughout
   477	\begin{equation}\label{eq:Hrange}
   478	  H_0\le H\le 2^{n/50},\qquad n\ge n_0,
   479	\end{equation}
   480	for absolute constants $H_0,n_0$.
   481	\begin{itemize}
   482	\item The weights are $V=\{(A,B)\in[1,H]^2:\gcd(A,B)=1,\ A\ne B\}$. For $H\ge H_0$ we have
   483	  $H^2/2\le|V|\le H^2$.
   484	\item The band scale is $Y=\lceil 2^{n/6}H^{-1/3}\rceil$.
   485	\item There are four bands, $k=0,1,2,3$:
   486	  \[\Pk=\{\ell\ \text{prime}:\ \ell\equiv3\pmod 4,\ 2^kY<\ell\le2^{k+1}Y\},\qquad
   487	  L_k=\prod_{\ell\in\Pk}\ell^2.\]
   488	\item The window bound is $\spanbar=\lfloor\tfrac52H(2^{n/2}+1)\rfloor+2H+3$, and
   489	  $\mbar=\lfloor\spanbar/(2^kY)^2\rfloor+2$.
   490	\item The base is $\beta=2$.
   491	\end{itemize}
   492	Under \eqref{eq:Hrange} the following scale separations hold:
   493	\begin{equation}\label{eq:scales}
   494	  Y\ge 16H^2,\qquad 16Y<P/2,\qquad Y^4>\sqrt2\,P,\qquad H\lceil\sqrt2P\rceil<Y^4.
   495	\end{equation}
   496	Indeed $H^{7/3}\le2^{7n/150}<2^{n/6}/32$, and $Y^4\ge2^{(2/3-4/150)n}>2^{n/2+1}H$.
   497	
   498	\subsection{Steps}
   499	Input: $N$ with $n$ bits.
   500	\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
   501	\item If $N<4$ output \texttt{FAIL}. If $N$ is even output $2$. Put
   502	  $a_0=\lceil\sqrt{N/2}\rceil$ and $b_0=\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor$. Every $(p,q)\in\Sn$ has
   503	  $p\in[a_0,b_0]$.
   504	\item For $v=(A,B)\in V$ let
   505	  \[F_v(x)=Bx+AN/x,\qquad \mathrm{Lo}_v=\lfloor2\sqrt{ABN}\rfloor,\qquad
   506	  \mathrm{Hi}_v=\max_{x\in\{a_0,b_0\}}\lceil F_v(x)\rceil.\]
   507	  \begin{itemize}
   508	  \item By AM--GM, $\mathrm{Lo}_v\le F_v(x)$ for all $x>0$.
   509	  \item By convexity, $F_v\le\mathrm{Hi}_v$ on $[a_0,b_0]$.
   510	  \item $\mathrm{Hi}_v-\mathrm{Lo}_v\le(B+\sqrt2A)\sqrt N+2H+2\le\spanbar$.
   511	  \end{itemize}
   512	\end{enumerate}
   513	Then, for each block $k=0,1,2,3$:
   514	\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.,resume]
   515	\item \emph{Exponent tree.} Build the balanced integer product tree of
   516	  $\{\ell^2:\ell\in\Pk\}$, and push $2$ down the tree: a node holding $2^{L_k/\Pi(\text{node})}$
   517	  passes $2^{L_k/\Pi(\text{node})\cdot\Pi(\text{sibling})}$ to each child, where $\Pi$ denotes
   518	  the product of the leaves below a node. This yields $u_\ell=2^{L_k/\ell^2}\bmod N$ for all
   519	  $\ell\in\Pk$. Put $\gamma=u_{\ell_0}^{\ell_0^2}=2^{L_k}\bmod N$. If some $\ell\in\Pk$ divides
   520	  $N$ and $1<\ell<N$, output $\ell$.
   521	\item \emph{Targets.} For $\ell\in\Pk$ and $v\in V$, let $a=ANB^{-1}\bmod\ell$. If
   522	  $a^{(\ell-1)/2}\not\equiv1\pmod\ell$, skip. Otherwise put $r_0=a^{(\ell+1)/4}\bmod\ell$; the
   523	  roots of $Br^2\equiv AN\pmod\ell$ are $r_0$ and $\ell-r_0$. For each root $r$ put
   524	  \[
   525	    s=Nr^{-1}\bmod\ell^2,\quad c=(Br+As)\bmod\ell^2,\quad
   526	    g_{\mathrm{lo}}=\Bigl\lceil\frac{\mathrm{Lo}_v-c}{\ell^2}\Bigr\rceil,\quad
   527	    g_{\mathrm{hi}}=\Bigl\lfloor\frac{\mathrm{Hi}_v-c}{\ell^2}\Bigr\rfloor,
   528	  \]
   529	  and $W_\tau=g_{\mathrm{hi}}-g_{\mathrm{lo}}+1\le\mbar$. The triple $\tau=(v,\ell,r)$ is a
   530	  \emph{target}. Let $T_k\le2|V||\Pk|$ be the number of targets. If $T_k=0$, skip the block.
   531	\item \emph{Baby length.} Choose $m$ either as $m=\mbar$, or by the \emph{endpoint-competitive}
   532	  rule: minimise $m+\sum_\tau\lceil W_\tau/m\rceil$ over an integer geometric grid whose last
   533	  point is $\max_\tau W_\tau$. Under either rule $m\le\mbar$ and $m+T'\le\mbar+T_k$, where
   534	  $T'=\sum_\tau\lceil W_\tau/m\rceil$.
   535	\item \emph{Giants.} Precompute $w_\ell=u_\ell^N$. For each target and
   536	  $0\le j<\lceil W_\tau/m\rceil$,
   537	  \[
   538	    G_{\tau,j}=w_\ell^{A}\,u_\ell^{\,B-c+\ell^2}\,\gamma^{-(g_{\mathrm{lo}}+1)}\,\gamma^{-jm}
   539	    \equiv u_\ell^{\,B+AN-c}\,\gamma^{-(g_{\mathrm{lo}}+jm)}\pmod N.
   540	  \]
   541	\item \emph{Collision detection.} Build $f(x)=\prod_{0\le i<m}(x-\gamma^i)\in(\Z/N\Z)[x]$ by a
   542	  product tree, and evaluate $f$ at all giants by a remainder tree. Only monic divisors occur, so
   543	  this is valid over $\Z/N\Z$. For each giant compute $\gcd(f(G),N)$, and output it if it lies
   544	  strictly between $1$ and $N$.
   545	\end{enumerate}
   546	If no block outputs a divisor, output \texttt{FAIL}.
   547	
   548	\begin{remark}\label{rem:alg}
   549	\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
   550	\item No multiplicative order is computed, assumed or certified, and no randomness is used.
   551	\item The weight $A=B$ is excluded. By Lemma~\ref{lem:modq} its true giant always collides
   552	  modulo $q$ too.
   553	\item One of the two rules in step~5 is mandatory for the cost bound. The bound $m\le\mbar$ alone
   554	  would allow $m=1$, making $T'$ too large.
   555	\item An implementation (Python, gmpy2, python-flint) is \texttt{src/dcltheorem/algorithm.py}.
   556	  Every output $x$ is checked for $1<x<N$ and $x\mid N$. An exhaustive regression covers all
   557	  $2\le N\le10^4$.
   558	\end{enumerate}
   559	\end{remark}
   560	
   561	%======================================================================
   562	\section{Analysis of one run}\label{sec:onerun}
   563	%======================================================================
   564	
   565	Throughout this section fix $(p,q)\in\Sn$, $N=pq$ and a block $k$. Write $L=L_k$, $u=u_\ell$ and
   566	$d=\ord_q(2)$. By \eqref{eq:scales} every band prime is smaller than both factors and exceeds $H$.
   567	
   568	\subsection{Lock, coverage and windows}
   569	
   570	\begin{lemma}[modulus-squared lock; {\cite[Prop.~3.3]{hh2022}}]\label{lem:lock}
   571	Let $\ell\in\Pk$, $(A,B)\in V$, and let $r$ be a root of $Br^2\equiv AN\pmod\ell$. If
   572	$p\equiv r\pmod\ell$, then $g:=(Bp+Aq-c)/\ell^2\in\Z$.
   573	\end{lemma}
   574	\begin{proof}
   575	$\ell\nmid ABN$. Write $p=r+\ell x$. Modulo $\ell^2$, $p^{-1}\equiv r^{-1}(1-\ell xr^{-1})$, so
   576	$q\equiv s-\ell x\,sr^{-1}$ and $Bp+Aq\equiv c+\ell x(B-Asr^{-1})$. Since $sr^{-1}\equiv Nr^{-2}$
   577	and $Br^2\equiv AN$ modulo $\ell$, the last term vanishes modulo $\ell^2$.
   578	\end{proof}
   579	
   580	\begin{lemma}[coverage criterion]\label{lem:coverage}
   581	$p\bmod\ell$ is one of the computed roots if and only if $\ell\mid Bp-Aq$. In that case the
   582	target $\tau=(v,\ell,p\bmod\ell)$ exists; we say that $\tau$ \emph{covers} $p$.
   583	\end{lemma}
   584	\begin{proof}
   585	$Bp^2-AN=p(Bp-Aq)$ and $\ell\nmid p$. If $\ell\mid Bp-Aq$ then $a\equiv p^2$ is a nonzero
   586	square, and for $\ell\equiv3\pmod4$ its roots are $\pm a^{(\ell+1)/4}$.
   587	\end{proof}
   588	
   589	\begin{lemma}[window]\label{lem:window}
   590	If $\tau$ covers $p$, then $g_{\mathrm{lo}}\le g\le g_{\mathrm{hi}}$. Put
   591	$i_{\mathrm{full}}=g-g_{\mathrm{lo}}$, $j^*=\lfloor i_{\mathrm{full}}/m\rfloor$ and
   592	$i^*=i_{\mathrm{full}}-j^*m$. Then $G_{\tau,j^*}$ is computed; we call it the \emph{true
   593	giant}.
   594	\end{lemma}
   595	\begin{proof}
   596	$\mathrm{Lo}_v\le F_v(p)=Bp+Aq\le\mathrm{Hi}_v$ because $p\in[a_0,b_0]$, and
   597	$i_{\mathrm{full}}\le W_\tau-1$.
   598	\end{proof}
   599	
   600	\subsection{Exact collision modulo \texorpdfstring{$p$}{p}; the clean band}
   601	
   602	\begin{lemma}[mod $p$]\label{lem:modp}
   603	If $\tau$ covers $p$, then $G_{\tau,j^*}\equiv\gamma^{i^*}\pmod p$. No hypothesis on any
   604	multiplicative order is needed.
   605	\end{lemma}
   606	\begin{proof}
   607	$B+AN-(Bp+Aq)=(p-1)(Aq-B)$, so $B+AN-c=\ell^2g+(p-1)(Aq-B)$. Since $2^{p-1}\equiv1\pmod p$,
   608	$u^{B+AN-c}\equiv2^{L g}=\gamma^g\pmod p$.
   609	\end{proof}
   610	
   611	\begin{lemma}[mod $q$]\label{lem:modq}
   612	If $\tau$ covers $p$, then $G_{\tau,j^*}\equiv\gamma^{i^*}u^{(A-B)(p-1)}\pmod q$.
   613	\end{lemma}
   614	\begin{proof}
   615	Modulo $q-1$ we have $N\equiv p$. So $B+AN-c\equiv B+Ap-c$, while $\ell^2g\equiv Bp+A-c$, and
   616	the difference is $(A-B)(p-1)$.
   617	\end{proof}
   618	
   619	\begin{definition}
   620	The true giant $G$ is \emph{FULL} if $G\equiv\gamma^{i'}\pmod q$ for some $i'\in[0,m)$.
   621	\end{definition}
   622	
   623	\begin{lemma}[one-sided extraction]\label{lem:extract}
   624	If $\tau$ covers $p$ and its true giant $G$ is not FULL, then $\gcd(f(G),N)=p$.
   625	\end{lemma}
   626	\begin{proof}
   627	The factor $i=i^*$ of $f(G)=\prod_{i<m}(G-\gamma^i)$ vanishes modulo $p$. Modulo $q$ no factor
   628	vanishes, and $\Z/q\Z$ is a field.
   629	\end{proof}
   630	
   631	\begin{lemma}[clean band]\label{lem:clean}
   632	Call block $k$ \emph{clean for $q$} if no $\ell\in\Pk$ divides $q-1$. At least one of the four
   633	blocks is clean for $q$.
   634	\end{lemma}
   635	\begin{proof}
   636	Otherwise $q-1$ has four distinct prime factors exceeding $Y$, so $q-1>Y^4>\sqrt2P>q$.
   637	\end{proof}
   638	
   639	\begin{lemma}[FULL on a clean block]\label{lem:full}
   640	If block $k$ is clean for $q$, then the true giant is FULL if and only if
   641	$d\mid(A-B)(p-1)-\ell^2\delta$ for some $\delta\in[-i^*,m-1-i^*]\subset(-m,m)$.
   642	\end{lemma}
   643	\begin{proof}
   644	FULL holds iff $2^{(L/\ell^2)[(A-B)(p-1)-\ell^2\delta]}\equiv1\pmod q$ for some
   645	$\delta=i'-i^*$. Since $d\mid q-1$ and $\gcd(L,q-1)=1$, the factor $L/\ell^2$ cancels.
   646	\end{proof}
   647	Only the enlarged range $|\delta|<m\le\mbar$ is used for counting below.
   648	
   649	\subsection{Counting lemmas}
   650	
   651	\begin{lemma}[small orders are rare]\label{lem:smallorder}
   652	For $Q_0\ge2$ and $T\ge1$,
   653	$\#\{q>Q_0\ \text{prime}:\ \ord_q(2)\le T\}\le T(T+1)/(2\log_2Q_0)$.
   654	\end{lemma}
   655	\begin{proof}
   656	Such a $q$ divides $2^j-1$ for some $j\le T$, and $2^j-1$ has fewer than $j/\log_2Q_0$ prime
   657	factors exceeding $Q_0$.
   658	\end{proof}
   659	
   660	\begin{lemma}[coverage by the large sieve]\label{lem:sieve}
   661	Fix a prime $q$ and a block $k$. Let $U_k(q)$ be the set of primes $p\in(P/2,P]$ with
   662	$\ell\nmid Bp-Aq$ for all $(v,\ell)\in V\times\Pk$. Then $|U_k(q)|\le CP/H^2$ for an absolute $C$.
   663	\end{lemma}
   664	\begin{proof}
   665	\begin{itemize}
   666	\item \emph{The forbidden classes.} For $\ell\in\Pk$, $\ell\mid Bp-Aq$ iff $p\equiv AqB^{-1}
   667	  \pmod\ell$. These $|V|$ classes are distinct, because $0<|AB'-A'B|<H^2<\ell$. So $U=U_k(q)$
   668	  avoids $|V|<\ell$ classes modulo every $\ell\in\Pk$. Let $Q=\lfloor P^{1/2}\rfloor$ and
   669	  $Z=\lfloor Q/2^{k+1}Y\rfloor$. Then $1\le Z<Y$ and $\ln Z\ge c''n$ by \eqref{eq:Hrange}. $U$
   670	  also avoids the class $0$ modulo every prime $r\le Z$, since $p>P/2>Z$.
   671	\item \emph{The large sieve.} Montgomery's arithmetic large sieve \cite{montgomery1968}, with the
   672	  constant of \cite{mv1973}, gives $|U|\le(P/2+1+Q^2)/J$, where
   673	  $J=\sum_{d\le Q}\mu^2(d)\prod_{r\mid d}\omega(r)/(r-\omega(r))$.
   674	\item \emph{Bounding $J$.} Keep only the terms $d=\ell a$ with $\ell\in\Pk$ and $a\le Z$
   675	  squarefree. These are squarefree and at most $Q$, because the primes of $a$ are below $Y<\ell$.
   676	  Each contributes $\frac{|V|}{\ell-|V|}\cdot\frac1{\varphi(a)}$. Using
   677	  $\sum_{a\le Z}\mu^2(a)/\varphi(a)\ge\sum_{m\le Z}1/m\ge\ln Z$, we get
   678	  $J\ge\lambda_k\ln Z$, where $\lambda_k:=|V|\sum_{\ell\in\Pk}1/\ell$.
   679	\item \emph{Conclusion.} By the prime number theorem in the progression $3\bmod4$,
   680	  $\sum_{\ell\in\Pk}1/\ell\ge c'/n$, so $\lambda_k\ge c'H^2/(2n)$. Hence
   681	  $|U|\le 2P/(\lambda_k c''n)\le CP/H^2$.
   682	\end{itemize}
   683	\end{proof}
   684	
   685	Using only the prime moduli $\ell$, as in the first version of this work, gives $CnP/H^2$
   686	instead. The refinement by the presieve over $r\le Z$ was pointed out by a GPT-6.1-sol referee.
   687	
   688	\begin{lemma}[FULL is rare]\label{lem:crt}
   689	Fix $q$ with $d=\ord_q(2)>T$ and a block $k$ clean for $q$. Let $F_k(q)$ be the set of integers
   690	$p\in[P/2,P]$ for which some $(v,\ell)\in V\times\Pk$ has $\ell\mid Bp-Aq$ and
   691	$d\mid(A-B)(p-1)-\ell^2\delta$ for some $|\delta|<\mbar$. Then
   692	\[
   693	  |F_k(q)|\le2|V||\Pk|\,\mbar\Bigl(\frac{PH}{2^{k+1}YT}+1\Bigr).
   694	\]
   695	\end{lemma}
   696	\begin{proof}
   697	Fix $(v,\ell,\delta)$ and let $g'=\gcd(A-B,d)\le H$. The congruence on $p$ is unsolvable or holds
   698	on one class modulo $d/g'$. Cleanliness gives $\ell\nmid d$. With $p\equiv AqB^{-1}\pmod\ell$, the
   699	CRT gives one class modulo $\ell d/g'\ge\ell T/H$. There are at most $|V||\Pk|(2\mbar-1)$ triples.
   700	\end{proof}

