   667	  \pmod\ell$. These $|V|$ classes are distinct, because $0<|AB'-A'B|<H^2<\ell$. So $U=U_k(q)$
   668	  avoids $|V|<\ell$ classes modulo every $\ell\in\Pk$. Let $Q=\lfloor P^{1/2}\rfloor$ and
   669	  $Z=\lfloor Q/2^{k+1}Y\rfloor$. Then $1\le Z<Y$ and $\ln Z\ge c''n$ by \eqref{eq:Hrange}. $U$
   670	  also avoids the class $0$ modulo every prime $r\le Z$, since $p>P/2>Z$.
   671	\item \emph{The large sieve.} Montgomery's arithmetic large sieve \cite{montgomery1968}, with the
   672	  constant of \cite{mv1973}, gives $|U|\le(P/2+1+Q^2)/J$, where
   673	  $J=\sum_{d\le Q}\mu^2(d)\prod_{r\mid d}\omega(r)/(r-\omega(r))$.
   674	\item \emph{Bounding $J$.} Keep only the terms $d=\ell a$ with $\ell\in\Pk$ and $a\le Z$
   675	  squarefree. These are squarefree and at most $Q$, because the primes of $a$ are below $Y<\ell$.
   676	  Each contributes $\frac{|V|}{\ell-|V|}\cdot\frac1{\varphi(a)}$. Using
   677	  $\sum_{a\le Z}\mu^2(a)/\varphi(a)\ge\sum_{m\le Z}1/m\ge\ln Z$, we get
   678	  $J\ge\lambda_k\ln Z$, where $\lambda_k:=|V|\sum_{\ell\in\Pk}1/\ell$.
   679	\item \emph{Conclusion.} By the prime number theorem in the progression $3\bmod4$,
   680	  $\sum_{\ell\in\Pk}1/\ell\ge c'/n$, so $\lambda_k\ge c'H^2/(2n)$. Hence
   681	  $|U|\le 2P/(\lambda_k c''n)\le CP/H^2$.
   682	\end{itemize}
   683	\end{proof}
   684	
   685	Using only the prime moduli $\ell$, as in the first version of this work, gives $CnP/H^2$
   686	instead. The refinement by the presieve over $r\le Z$ was pointed out by a GPT-6.1-sol referee.
   687	
   688	\begin{lemma}[FULL is rare]\label{lem:crt}
   689	Fix $q$ with $d=\ord_q(2)>T$ and a block $k$ clean for $q$. Let $F_k(q)$ be the set of integers
   690	$p\in[P/2,P]$ for which some $(v,\ell)\in V\times\Pk$ has $\ell\mid Bp-Aq$ and
   691	$d\mid(A-B)(p-1)-\ell^2\delta$ for some $|\delta|<\mbar$. Then
   692	\[
   693	  |F_k(q)|\le2|V||\Pk|\,\mbar\Bigl(\frac{PH}{2^{k+1}YT}+1\Bigr).
   694	\]
   695	\end{lemma}
   696	\begin{proof}
   697	Fix $(v,\ell,\delta)$ and let $g'=\gcd(A-B,d)\le H$. The congruence on $p$ is unsolvable or holds
   698	on one class modulo $d/g'$. Cleanliness gives $\ell\nmid d$. With $p\equiv AqB^{-1}\pmod\ell$, the
   699	CRT gives one class modulo $\ell d/g'\ge\ell T/H$. There are at most $|V||\Pk|(2\mbar-1)$ triples.
   700	\end{proof}
   701	
   702	\subsection{One run}
   703	For $T\ge1$ (an analysis-only parameter) and $(p,q)\in\Sn$, let $k(q)$ be the least block clean
   704	for $q$. Define
   705	\[
   706	  E_a(T)=\{d\le T\},\quad E_b(H)=\{d>T,\ p\in U_{k(q)}(q)\},\quad
   707	  E_c(H,T)=\{d>T,\ p\in F_{k(q)}(q)\}.
   708	\]
   709	
   710	\begin{proposition}[one run; proved]\label{prop:onerun}
   711	There are absolute constants $C,c$ such that for $H$ in the range \eqref{eq:Hrange} and
   712	$1\le T\le P/2$ the following hold.
   713	\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
   714	\item $\DCL_{n,H}$ outputs a proper divisor of $N$ or \texttt{FAIL} on every input.
   715	\item It costs at most $Cn^cH^{5/3}2^{n/6}$ bit operations on every $n$-bit input.
   716	\item Every \texttt{FAIL} pair of $\Sn$ lies in $E_a(T)\cup E_b(H)\cup E_c(H,T)$, and
   717	  \[
   718	  \frac{|E_a|}{|\Sn|}\le\frac{CnT^2}{P},\quad \frac{|E_b|}{|\Sn|}\le\frac{Cn}{H^2},\quad
   719	  \frac{|E_c|}{|\Sn|}\le Cn\Bigl(\frac{H^{14/3}P^{1/3}}{T}+\frac{H^{10/3}}{P^{1/3}}\Bigr).
   720	  \]
   721	\end{enumerate}
   722	\end{proposition}
   723	\begin{proof}
   724	\begin{itemize}
   725	\item \emph{Success certificate.} Suppose $d>T$, $p\notin U_{k(q)}(q)$ and
   726	  $p\notin F_{k(q)}(q)$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:coverage} some target of block $k(q)$ covers $p$, and
   727	  by Lemma~\ref{lem:window} its true giant is computed. By Lemma~\ref{lem:full} it is not FULL,
   728	  since the exact $\delta$-range lies in $|\delta|<\mbar$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:extract} its gcd is
   729	  $p$. So some block outputs a proper divisor.
   730	\item \emph{Normalisation.} There are at most $3P/n$ primes $q\in(P/\sqrt2,\sqrt2P)$, and we
   731	  divide by $|\Sn|\ge c_0P^2/n^2$. This needs no prime counts in short intervals.
   732	\item \emph{$E_a$.} By Lemma~\ref{lem:smallorder} with $Q_0=2^{(n-1)/2}$, at most
   733	  $T(T+1)/(n-1)$ primes $q$ qualify, each with at most $P/2+1$ partners.
   734	\item \emph{$E_b$.} This follows from Lemma~\ref{lem:sieve}.
   735	\item \emph{$E_c$.} Use $|V|\le H^2$, $|\Pk|\le2^kY$ and $\mbar\le3HP/(4^kY^2)$ in
   736	  Lemma~\ref{lem:crt}. This gives $|F_k(q)|\le6H^4P^2/(Y^2T)+6H^3P/Y$. Then sum over $q$ and use
   737	  $Y\ge P^{1/3}H^{-1/3}$.
   738	\item \emph{(i).} Only validated gcds and band primes are output.
   739	\item \emph{(ii).} See \S\ref{sec:cost}.
   740	\end{itemize}
   741	\end{proof}
   742	
   743	\begin{remark}[Theorem D$'$]\label{rem:Dprime}
   744	Take $H=\lceil n2^{\varepsilon n/2}\rceil$ and $T=\lfloor2^{(1/4-\varepsilon)n}\rfloor$. Then
   745	Proposition~\ref{prop:onerun} gives cost $N^{1/6+5\varepsilon/6+o(1)}$ and exceptions
   746	$O(N^{-\varepsilon})$ for every fixed $\varepsilon<1/52$. The binding term is the first $E_c$
   747	term, which needs $-1/12+13\varepsilon/3<0$. This is Theorem~D$'$ of the project notes, stated
   748	there for $\varepsilon\le1/60$.
   749	
   750	A dyadic recount of $E_c$ over the size of $\ord_q(2)$ removes $E_a$ and certifies the unchanged
   751	algorithm for every $\varepsilon<1/40$. Combining D$'$ at $\varepsilon=1/55$ with a single
   752	Harvey--Hittmeir fallback gives average cost $N^{2/11+o(1)}$ on $\Sn$. This was observed by a
   753	GPT-6.1-sol referee and is superseded by Theorem~\ref{thm:AV}. (The dyadic recount and the
   754	$2/11$ claim are proved in \texttt{notes/avgtime\_results.md}, Lemma~D and \S4. Neither is used
   755	below.)
   756	\end{remark}
   757	
   758	\subsection{Bit complexity}\label{sec:cost}
   759	Per block, with $T_k$ targets and $m\le\mbar$, the costs are as follows.
   760	
   761	\begin{center}
   762	\small
   763	\begin{tabular}{p{0.42\textwidth}p{0.22\textwidth}p{0.24\textwidth}}
   764	\toprule
   765	step & operations & bit cost\\
   766	\midrule
   767	prime sieve to $16Y$; $V$; $\mathrm{Lo}_v,\mathrm{Hi}_v$ & $O(Y\log\log Y)$; $O(H^2)$ square roots & $\tilde O(Y+H^2n)$\\
   768	integer product tree of $\{\ell^2\}$ ($\log_2L_k\le3\cdot2^kY$) & balanced tree & $O(M(Y)\log Y)$\\
   769	exponent tree; $\gamma,\gamma^{-1}$ & $O(Y\log Y)$ mults mod $N$, 1 inversion & $O(Y\log Y\,M(n))$\\
   770	roots, $s$, $c$, windows & $O(|V||\Pk|\log Y)$ mults mod $\ell,\ell^2$ & $\tilde O(H^2Y)$\\
   771	$w_\ell=u_\ell^N$; giants & $O(n|\Pk|+nT_k+T')$ mults mod $N$ & $O((n(T_k+Y)+T')M(n))$\\
   772	choice of $m$; small inversions & $O(nT_k)$ small-integer ops & $\tilde O(T_kn^{O(1)})$\\
   773	babies $\gamma^i$ & $m$ mults & $O(mM(n))$\\
   774	$f$ by product tree; remainder tree at $T'$ giants & $O((M(m)+M(T'))\log(m+T'))$ ring ops & $\tilde O((m+T')n)$\\
   775	gcds & $T'$ gcds of $n$-bit integers & $O(T'M(n)\log n)$\\
   776	\bottomrule
   777	\end{tabular}
   778	\end{center}
   779	
   780	\begin{itemize}
   781	\item Polynomial products over $\Z/N\Z$ use Kronecker substitution with slots wider than
   782	  $2n+\lceil\log_2(D+1)\rceil$ \cite{vzgg}.
   783	\item Remainders by monic polynomials use reversal and Newton inversion of a power series with
   784	  constant term $1$. So no inverse of a nonunit is ever needed.
   785	\item By step~5, $m+T'\le\mbar+T_k\le\tfrac52HP/Y^2+4+2H^2\cdot2^{k+1}Y$.
   786	\item Summing over the four blocks gives $O(n^{O(1)}(HP/Y^2+H^2Y))=O(n^{O(1)}H^{5/3}P^{1/3})$,
   787	  since $Y\asymp P^{1/3}H^{-1/3}$. This proves Proposition~\ref{prop:onerun}(ii).
   788	\end{itemize}
   789	
   790	%======================================================================
   791	\section{A fourth-moment coverage bound}\label{sec:fourth}
   792	%======================================================================
   793	
   794	The large sieve bounds the uncovered fraction by $O(1/\lambda)$, where $\lambda\asymp H^2/n$ is
   795	the expected number of covering events. Here we average over both $p$ and $q$ and control the
   796	joint statistics of up to four band primes. This gives $O(\lambda^{-2})$.
   797	
   798	\subsection{Setting}
   799	\begin{itemize}
   800	\item Let $\Xb\ge1$ be an integer and $\mathfrak B_0=(0,\Xb]^2\cap\Z^2$.
   801	\item Let $\mathcal P$ be a nonempty set of primes in $(Y,2Y]$, and $V$ a nonempty set of
   802	  coprime pairs $(A,B)\in[1,H]^2$.
   803	\item Put $s=\sum_{\ell\in\mathcal P}1/\ell$ and $\lambda=|V|s$.
   804	\item For $x=(a,b)$ and $v=(A,B)$ put $F_v(x)=Ba-Ab$, and
   805	  $z_\ell(x)=\#\{v\in V:\ell\mid F_v(x)\}$, $\mathcal W(x)=\#\{\ell\in\mathcal P:z_\ell(x)>0\}$.
   806	  The point $x$ is \emph{uncovered} if $\mathcal W(x)=0$.
   807	\item Let $\mathcal L=\bigcup_{v\in V}\R v$ and $\mathfrak B=\mathfrak B_0\setminus\mathcal L$.
   808	  A prime pair $p\ne q$ with $p,q>H$ never lies on $\mathcal L$.
   809	\item A \emph{configuration} of order $j$ is $\mathbf c=((\ell_1,v_1),\dots,(\ell_j,v_j))$ with
   810	  distinct $\ell_i\in\mathcal P$ and $v_i\in V$. Its lattice is
   811	  $\Lambda_{\mathbf c}=\{x:\ell_i\mid F_{v_i}(x)\ \forall i\}$, and
   812	  $d_{\mathbf c}=\prod\ell_i$.
   813	\end{itemize}
   814	
   815	\begin{theorem}[two-variable fourth moment; proved]\label{thm:fourth}
   816	Assume $H\ge2$, $Y\ge16H^2$, $H\Xb<Y^4$ and $\lambda\ge1$. Then
   817	\[
   818	  \#\{x\in\mathfrak B:\mathcal W(x)=0\}\le \Xb^2\Bigl(\frac3{\lambda^2}+\frac1{\lambda^3}\Bigr)
   819	  +2H\Xb+\lambda^{-4}\sum_{j=1}^4\kappa_j\mathrm{Err}_j,
   820	\]
   821	with $\kappa_1=15\lambda^3$, $\kappa_2=25\lambda^2$, $\kappa_3=10\lambda$, $\kappa_4=1$ and
   822	$\mathrm{Err}_j=A_j+B_j+C_j+D_j+E_j+F_j$, where:
   823	\begin{itemize}
   824	\item $A_j=(|\mathcal P||V|)^j$;
   825	\item $B_j=28.3\,\Xb(2Y)^{j/2}(2|V|)^j$;
   826	\item $C_j=2H\Xb\sum_{r=\lceil j/2\rceil}^{j}\binom jr\lambda^r|\mathcal P|^{j-r}$;
   827	\item $D_j=\Xb^2|V|s^4$ if $j=4$, and $D_j=0$ otherwise;
   828	\item $E_j=6H\Xb\sum_{r\in I_j}\binom jr\lambda^r|\mathcal P|^{j-r}
   829	  +2H\Xb|V|\sum_{r\in I_j}\binom jrs^{j-r}(|\mathcal P||V|)^r$, with
   830	  $I_j=[\max(0,j-3),\lfloor j/2\rfloor]$;
   831	\item $F_j=(3|V|)^j\Xb^2s/Y$.
   832	\end{itemize}
   833	\end{theorem}
   834	
   835	\subsubsection{Elementary facts}
   836	\begin{enumerate}[label=(F\arabic*)]
   837	\item If $\ell\in\mathcal P$ does not divide both coordinates of $x$, then $z_\ell(x)\le1$.
   838	  Two events give $\ell\mid(AB'-A'B)a$ and $\ell\mid(AB'-A'B)b$, with $0<|AB'-A'B|<H^2<\ell$.
   839	\item For $x\in\mathfrak B$, every $F_v(x)$ is a nonzero integer with $|F_v(x)|<H\Xb<Y^4$. So it
   840	  has at most $3$ prime factors in $(Y,2Y]$, and $\sum_\ell z_\ell(x)\le3|V|$.
   841	\item $\Lambda_{\mathbf c}$ has index $d_{\mathbf c}$, by the CRT, since $x\mapsto F_{v_i}(x)$
   842	  maps onto $\Z/\ell_i$.
   843	\item For $v\in V$ let $r_v(\mathbf c)=\#\{i:v_i\ne v\}$ and $t_v(\mathbf c)=\prod_{v_i\ne
   844	  v}\ell_i$. Then $\Lambda_{\mathbf c}\cap\R v=\Z t_vv$, since $0<|B_iA-A_iB|<\ell_i$ when
   845	  $v_i\ne v$.
   846	\item The shortest vector $u_{\mathbf c}$ of $\Lambda_{\mathbf c}$ has
   847	  $|u_{\mathbf c}|\le1.0746\sqrt{d_{\mathbf c}}$ (Hermite's constant in dimension two).
   848	\end{enumerate}
   849	
   850	\begin{lemma}[lattice points in a box]\label{lem:L}
   851	For a lattice $\Lambda\subset\R^2$ of covolume $d$ with shortest vector length $\rho$, and a
   852	convex $K$ of diameter $D$,
   853	$|\#(\Lambda\cap K)-\mathrm{area}(K)/d|\le1+(4/\sqrt3)D/\rho$.
   854	\end{lemma}
   855	\begin{proof}
   856	Take a Lagrange--Gauss reduced basis $b_1,b_2$, so that $d\ge(\sqrt3/2)|b_1||b_2|$. In these
   857	coordinates $K$ becomes a convex $K'$ of area $\mathrm{area}(K)/d$. Its widths in the two
   858	coordinates are at most $(2/\sqrt3)D/\rho$. Count $\Z^2\cap K'$ column by column. Each column
   859	errs by at most $1$, and the column lengths are unimodal, so the column sum errs by at most the
   860	other width.
   861	\end{proof}
   862	
   863	\begin{lemma}[one configuration]\label{lem:conf}
   864	Let $\mathbf c$ have order $j\le4$, $d=d_{\mathbf c}$ and $u=u_{\mathbf c}$.
   865	\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
   866	\item If $u\notin\mathcal L$, then
   867	  $|\#(\Lambda_{\mathbf c}\cap\mathfrak B)-\Xb^2/d|\le1+3.266\Xb/|u|+\sum_{v'}\Xb/(t_{v'}\max(A',B'))$,
   868	  where the sum runs over $v'$ with $r_{v'}(\mathbf c)\ge\lceil j/2\rceil$.
   869	\item Otherwise $u=\pm t_v v$ for one $v\in V$. Put $r=r_v(\mathbf c)$ and $g=d/t_v$; then
   870	  $r\le\lfloor j/2\rfloor$. If $j-r\ge4$, then $\Lambda_{\mathbf c}\cap\mathfrak B=\emptyset$. If
   871	  $j-r\le3$, then
   872	  \[
   873	  |\#(\Lambda_{\mathbf c}\cap\mathfrak B)-\Xb^2/d|\le\frac{2\sqrt2\Xb}{t_v|v|}+\frac{2H\Xb}{g}
   874	  +\sum_{v'\ne v}\frac{\Xb}{t_{v'}\max(A',B')},
   875	  \]
   876	  where again only $v'$ with $r_{v'}(\mathbf c)\ge\lceil j/2\rceil$ occur.
   877	\end{enumerate}
   878	\end{lemma}
   879	\begin{proof}
   880	\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
   881	\item \emph{Directions $v'$ not parallel to $u$.} The vector $t_{v'}v'\in\Lambda_{\mathbf c}$ is
   882	  independent of $u$, so $|t_{v'}v'|\ge d/|u|\ge0.93Y^{j/2}$. On the other hand
   883	  $|t_{v'}v'|\le(2Y)^{r_{v'}}\sqrt2H$. If $r_{v'}\le\lceil j/2\rceil-1$, this contradicts
   884	  $Y\ge16H^2$ for $j\le4$. (The tightest case is $j=3$, $r=1$, which would need
   885	  $\sqrt Y\le3.3H$.) The points of $\Lambda_{\mathbf c}\cap\mathfrak B_0$ on $\R v'$ are
   886	  $\sigma t_{v'}v'$ with $1\le\sigma\le\Xb/(t_{v'}\max(A',B'))$.
   887	\item \emph{Case (i).} Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:L} to $\mathfrak B_0$ (diameter $\sqrt2\Xb$), and
   888	  subtract the points on $\mathcal L$ counted in (a).
   889	\item \emph{Case (ii), $r\le\lfloor j/2\rfloor$.} We have $|u|=t_v|v|\ge Y^r$ and
   890	  $|u|\le1.0746(2Y)^{j/2}$. If $r>j/2$, then $Y^{1/2}\le4.3$, a contradiction.
   891	\item \emph{Case (ii), line structure.} For $x\in\Lambda_{\mathbf c}$, $\det(v,x)\in g\Z$.
   892	  Each line $\{\det(v,\cdot)=mg\}$ meets $\Lambda_{\mathbf c}$ in a coset of $\Z u$. On
   893	  $\mathfrak B_0$, $|\det(v,x)|<H\Xb$.
   894	  \begin{itemize}
   895	  \item If $j-r\ge4$, then $g>Y^4>H\Xb$, so only $m=0$ occurs. That line is $\R v\subset\mathcal
   896	    L$, which is removed.
   897	  \item If $j-r\le3$, let $h(\sigma)$ be the length of the chord $\{y\in[0,\Xb]^2:\det(v,y)=\sigma\}$.
   898	    It is concave, $\max h\le\sqrt2\Xb$, and $\int h=|v|\Xb^2$. Summing $h(mg)/|u|\pm1$ over the
   899	    at most $2H\Xb/g$ nonzero $m$, and using $g|u|=d|v|$, gives the bound. Then subtract the
   900	    other lines of $\mathcal L$ as in (a).
   901	  \end{itemize}
   902	\end{enumerate}
   903	\end{proof}
   904	
   905	\subsubsection{Summation and assembly}
   906	Summing Lemma~\ref{lem:conf} over all configurations of order $j$ gives
   907	\[
   908	  \sum_{\mathbf c}\bigl|\#(\Lambda_{\mathbf c}\cap\mathfrak B)-\Xb^2/d_{\mathbf c}\bigr|\le
   909	  A_j+B_j+C_j+D_j+E_j.
   910	\]
   911	The rows arise as follows.
   912	\begin{itemize}
   913	\item \emph{$A_j$.} This is the ``$+1$'' term, summed over at most $(|\mathcal P||V|)^j$
   914	  configurations.
   915	\item \emph{$B_j$.} In case (i) a configuration is counted at its own shortest vector $u$, which
   916	  has $|u|\le R_j:=1.0746(2Y)^{j/2}$. Such a $u$ has $|F_v(u)|\le6.1HY^2<Y^3$, so it satisfies at
   917	  most $2|V|$ events and lies in at most $(2|V|)^j$ lattices. Then use
   918	  $\sum_{0<|u|\le R}1/|u|\le8R$.
   919	\item \emph{$C_j$ and $E_j$.} Swap the sums over configurations and directions. For fixed $v'$
   920	  and $r$, $\sum_{\mathbf c:r_{v'}=r}1/t_{v'}\le\binom jr\lambda^r|\mathcal P|^{j-r}$. Also
   921	  $\sum_{v'}1/\max(A',B')<2H$ and $\sum_v2\sqrt2/|v|\le6H$. The $2H\Xb/g$ terms contribute
   922	  $\sum_{\mathbf c:r_v=r}1/g\le\binom jrs^{j-r}(|\mathcal P||V|)^r$.
   923	\item \emph{$D_j$.} Case (ii) with $j-r\ge4$ occurs only for $j=4$, $r=0$, with error
   924	  $\Xb^2/d$ summing to $\Xb^2|V|s^4$.
   925	\end{itemize}
   926	
   927	Put $F_j(x):=\sum_{\mathbf c}1[x\in\Lambda_{\mathbf c}]$. By (F1),
   928	$F_j(x)=(\mathcal W(x))_j$, the falling factorial, unless $x$ lies in
   929	$\mathcal D=\{x:\exists\ell\in\mathcal P,\ \ell\mid a,\ \ell\mid b\}$. On $\mathfrak B$, both
   930	quantities lie in $[0,(3|V|)^j]$ by (F2), and $\#\mathcal D\cap\mathfrak B_0\le\Xb^2s/Y$. Hence
   931	\[
   932	  \Bigl|\sum_{\mathfrak B}(\mathcal W)_j-\Xb^2\,\mathbb E(\mathcal Z)_j\Bigr|\le\mathrm{Err}_j.
   933	\]
   934	Here $\mathcal Z=\sum_\ell\mathcal Z_\ell$ with independent
   935	$\mathcal Z_\ell\sim\mathrm{Bernoulli}(|V|/\ell)$, so that
   936	$\mathbb E(\mathcal Z)_j=\sum_{\mathbf c}1/d_{\mathbf c}$.
   937	
   938	Now expand
   939	\[
   940	(w-\lambda)^4=(w)_4+(6-4\lambda)(w)_3+(7-12\lambda+6\lambda^2)(w)_2+(1-4\lambda+6\lambda^2-4\lambda^3)(w)_1+\lambda^4 .
   941	\]
   942	For $\lambda\ge1$ the coefficients are bounded by the $\kappa_j$. The same identity holds for
   943	$\mathcal Z$, and $\mathbb E(\mathcal Z-\lambda)^4\le\lambda+3\lambda^2$. Finally
   944	$\#\mathfrak B\le\Xb^2$, and each uncovered point contributes $\lambda^4$. So
   945	\[
   946	  \lambda^4\#\{\mathcal W=0\}\le\Xb^2(3\lambda^2+\lambda)+2H\Xb\lambda^4+\sum_j\kappa_j\mathrm{Err}_j,
   947	\]
   948	which is Theorem~\ref{thm:fourth}. Every displayed inequality was also checked exhaustively on
   949	small boxes by code (\texttt{tests/test\_sieve2d\_bound.py}). \qed
   950	
   951	\subsection{Consequences}
   952	
   953	\begin{theorem}[coverage exceptional set; proved]\label{thm:Eb4}
   954	In the setting of Proposition~\ref{prop:onerun}, and with $C$ absolute,
   955	\[
   956	  \frac{|E_b(H)|}{|\Sn|}\le C\Bigl(\frac{n^4}{H^4}+n^6H^{1/3}N^{-1/6}\Bigr).
   957	\]
   958	\end{theorem}
   959	\begin{proof}
   960	$E_b$ is contained in the union over the blocks $k$ of the pairs uncovered by block $k$. Prime
   961	pairs of $\Sn$ lie in $\mathfrak B$ with $\Xb=\lceil\sqrt2P\rceil$. The hypotheses of
   962	Theorem~\ref{thm:fourth} hold for each block with $Y_k=2^kY$, by \eqref{eq:scales}. We have
   963	$\lambda_k\ge c_1H^2/n$, and we may assume $\lambda_k\ge1$. The normalised error terms are then:
   964	\begin{itemize}
   965	\item $HY_k^3/(|V|^3\Xb)=O(H^{-6})$, using $Y^3H\le2P$;
   966	\item $Y_k^2/(\Xb s^4)=O(n^4P^{-1/3}H^{-2/3})$;
   967	\item $1/(Y_ks^3)=O(n^3P^{-1/3}H^{1/3})$;
   968	\item the rest are smaller.
   969	\end{itemize}
   970	Dividing by $|\Sn|\ge c_0P^2/n^2$ gives the bound.
   971	\end{proof}
   972	
   973	\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:D}]
   974	\begin{itemize}
   975	\item Correctness and cost come from Proposition~\ref{prop:onerun}(i)--(ii).
   976	\item \emph{(a).} Take $H=\lceil n2^{\varepsilon n/4}\rceil$ and
   977	  $T=\lfloor2^{(1/4-\varepsilon/2)n}/n\rfloor$. Then $|E_a|/|\Sn|=O(nT^2/P)=O(2^{-\varepsilon n}/n)$.
