   987	\subsection{Where this method stops}
   988	The following remark explains why the method stops at $\lambda^{-2}$.
   989	
   990	\begin{remark}[a moment barrier; proved for its stated scope]\label{rem:PYY}
   991	Fix $J\ge2$. Peled, Yadin and Yehudayoff \cite[Cor.~1.2(1), 1.3(1)]{pyy2011} construct $J$-wise
   992	independent Bernoulli variables with prescribed marginals and $\Pr(\text{all }0)\ge
   993	c_J\lambda^{-\lfloor J/2\rfloor}$. Attach to each event a uniform label in $V$. Then all labelled
   994	event statistics of order at most $J$ agree exactly with the independent model, while the
   995	uncovered mass is $\ge c_J\lambda^{-\lfloor J/2\rfloor}$. So no argument whose only input is the
   996	joint distribution of at most $J$ band-prime events can prove $o(\lambda^{-\lfloor J/2\rfloor})$.
   997	
   998	Our error analysis controls $J\le4$; $J=5$ is sketched in the notes, and by the above it cannot
   999	improve on $\lambda^{-2}$. At $J=6$ the per-lattice error bounds add up to about $\Xb^2/H^2$,
  1000	far above the target $\Xb^2\lambda^{-3}$. This is a limit of this proof technique, not of the
  1001	truth. Exploratory sixth moments agree with the independent model to within $4\%$
  1002	(Section~\ref{sec:experiments}). A Poisson-quality bound $e^{-c\lambda}$ would need genuinely
  1003	new arithmetic input. Any such proof must also exclude integer dilates $t(H,H+1)$, which are
  1004	uncovered integer points.
  1005	\end{remark}
