  1384	\begin{proof}
  1385	\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
  1386	\item \emph{Sum by keys.} Both primes exceed $K$, so they are units modulo $K$, and each $x$ has
  1387	  one partner class $\kappa x^{-1}$ per unit key $\kappa$. Let $\varphi(\kappa)$ denote the bound
  1388	  of Lemma~\ref{lem:partner}. Then
  1389	  \[
  1390	  \mathrm{Count}(\iota)\le\sum_\kappa\varphi(\kappa)\,\#\{x:x\bmod M\in R(\kappa)\},
  1391	  \]
  1392	  and the last factor is at most $c_s|R(\kappa)|P/M$, with $c_s=1$ on side $p$ and $5/4$ on side
  1393	  $q$.
  1394	\item \emph{Bound $\varphi$.} Using $\min(u,v)\le\sqrt{uv}$ and $K\le P/2$,
  1395	  $\varphi(\kappa)\le\sqrt{2P|S(\kappa)|(D/|A|+K)}/K$.
  1396	\item \emph{Pass to $\mathbb E_N$.} Every residue class modulo $K$ contains at least
  1397	  $2^{n-2}/K$ integers of $[2^{n-1},2^n)$. So $\sum_\kappa f(\kappa)\le2K\,\mathbb E_Nf(N\bmod
  1398	  K)$ for $f\ge0$. With (L1), $\sqrt{D/|A|+K}\le M\sqrt{C_1+C_2}$, which gives
  1399	  \[
  1400	  \mathrm{Count}(\iota)\le c_1P^{3/2}\,\mathbb E_N\bigl[|R(N\bmod K)|\,|S(N\bmod K)|^{1/2}\bigr].
  1401	  \]
  1402	\item \emph{$A=0$.} Swap sides: put $R'(\kappa)=\{\kappa r^{-1}\}$ and $(A',B')=(B,0)$. The
  1403	  coverage is identical and $A'\ne0$.
  1404	\item \emph{Sum over the design and apply the budget.} Summing over generators gives
  1405	  $\mathbb E_N\sum_\tau|S_\tau|^{1/2}$, which is at most
  1406	  $\sqrt{\mathbb E\#\tau\cdot\mathbb E\sum|S_\tau|}\le X^{3/2}$ by Cauchy--Schwarz. Finally
  1407	  divide by $|\Sn|\ge c_0P^2/n^2$.
  1408	\end{enumerate}
  1409	\end{proof}
  1410	
  1411	The proof counts integer pairs and needs no flatness, height or prime equidistribution.
  1412	Arbitrary finite windows are allowed: a sparse window counts by its cardinality, not its hull.
  1413	This is what also covers searches over several rational bases with known multiplicative
  1414	relations, after a reduction to prime-valuation coordinates (\texttt{notes/below16\_results.md},
  1415	Prop.~MB).
  1416	
  1417	\begin{proposition}[the search lies in the model; proved]\label{prop:Dinmodel}
  1418	$\DCL_{n,H}$ is dominated, at constant-factor cost, by a design satisfying (L1) with $M=K=\ell$
  1419	and $D=\ell^2$. Hence any design in the model that succeeds on a constant fraction of $\Sn$ costs
  1420	at least $N^{1/6}/(\log N)^{O(1)}$, and Theorem~\ref{thm:D}(b) is within a polylogarithmic factor
  1421	of this.
  1422	\end{proposition}
  1423	\begin{proof}
  1424	The targets are $(A,B,\ell,\text{root})$. The class set is the set of roots of $Br^2\equiv AN$,
  1425	whose key is $N\bmod\ell$. Replace each window by the $n$-only superset
  1426	$[\lfloor F_{\min}/\ell^2\rfloor-1,\lfloor F_{\max}/\ell^2\rfloor+1]$ over all $n$-bit $N$. Its
  1427	span is at most $2.12HP<\spanbar$, so each width is at most $\mbar+1$. Then
  1428	$\sum|S|\le\sum_kT_k(\mbar+1)\le(\sum_k(\mbar+T_k))^2$, the square of the list cost.
  1429	\end{proof}
  1430	
  1431	\begin{remark}[what the model does not cover]\label{rem:T1scope}
  1432	The model leaves out several things.
  1433	\begin{itemize}
  1434	\item \emph{Partial variants.} A second theorem T2 allows about $n/2$ bits of size information
  1435	  about $N$ with interval windows and gives the same bound (\texttt{notes/below16\_results.md}
  1436	  \S2.2). Targets of degree $\ge2$ give $N^{1/5}$ on average, conditionally on a real-fibre
  1437	  hypothesis (ibid., Cor.~F).
  1438	\item \emph{Excluded designs.} Not covered are keys finer than $C_2M^2$, sparse windows combined
  1439	  with size information, several labels per giant, and global selectors that localise $p$
  1440	  cheaply.
  1441	\item \emph{Smooth-order methods.} These lie outside the model. Pollard's $p-1$ method factors a
  1442	  positive proportion of nearly balanced semiprimes at cost $N^{0.152}$
  1443	  \cite[Prop.~35.3]{wardmaestro2026}, below the floor. By the same report (remarks after
  1444	  Prop.~35.3) it fails on a positive proportion for every smoothness bound up to $N^{0.33}$, so it
  1445	  gives no almost-all statement.
  1446	\item \emph{Relation methods.} A deterministic quadratic sieve would be heuristically
  1447	  subexponential on $\Sn$. Proving this needs smooth-value concentration and a nontrivial Jacobi
  1448	  character on the relation kernel, neither of which is known.
  1449	\end{itemize}
  1450	\end{remark}
  1451	
