  1453	\section{Unbalanced ratios [PLACEHOLDER]}\label{sec:unbalanced}
  1454	%======================================================================
  1455	
  1456	\begin{center}
  1457	\fbox{\parbox{0.92\textwidth}{\small\textbf{Placeholder.} The project's analysis of unbalanced
  1458	ratios (\texttt{notes/unbalanced\_results.md}) is still in progress and not yet reviewed. This
  1459	subsection records only the known cost envelope of the \emph{proposed} scheme. It will be
  1460	replaced when the final result is available.}}
  1461	\end{center}
  1462	
  1463	\subsection{Unbalanced ratios}
  1464	Write $q/p=N^\theta$, $0\le\theta\le1/3$. The proposed localised-weight scheme uses weights
  1465	$(1,B)$ with $B$ up to $H^2$ and per-band prime scales $Y\asymp(N^{1+\theta})^{1/6}H^{-2/3}$.
  1466	A cost analysis of that scheme, combined with Pollard--Strassen for the smaller factor
  1467	$p\approx N^{(1-\theta)/2}$, gives the cost exponent
  1468	\[
  1469	  e(\theta)=\min\Bigl(\frac{1+\theta}6,\ \frac{1-\theta}4\Bigr)\quad(+O(\varepsilon)\text{ on
  1470	  the first branch}).
  1471	\]
  1472	This exceeds $1/6$ for $0<\theta<1/3$ and reaches its maximum $1/5$ at $\theta=1/5$, i.e.\ at
  1473	$p\approx N^{2/5}$. This is a \emph{cost analysis of the proposed scheme}, not a proved theorem
  1474	of this paper. Ratios $q/p\le N^{o(1)}$ are expected to keep exponent $1/6$. A uniform-in-ratio
  1475	statement below $1/5$ is not claimed.
  1476	
