  1562	%======================================================================
  1563	\section{Limitations and open problems}\label{sec:open}
  1564	%======================================================================
  1565	
  1566	\begin{itemize}
  1567	\item \textbf{Worst case.} By Theorem~\ref{thm:R}, what is missing is coverage alone. Prove
  1568	  Hypothesis~\ref{hyp:DCHpair} for some $\kappa$, or any covering theorem with $c>3/8$. The
  1569	  first test case is a covering statement for the union of short intervals
  1570	  $\{bx-a\}$ with $x\asymp Y^3$ and height $Y^{1/8}$.
  1571	\item \textbf{Exceptional rate.} The proved tails are $n/H^2$ and $n^4/H^4$. The data support
  1572	  $e^{-cH^2/n}$. Remark~\ref{rem:PYY} shows that this needs information beyond five-prime
  1573	  statistics.
  1574	\item \textbf{Below $1/6$.} Theorem~\ref{thm:T1} rules this out for residue-local degree-one
  1575	  collision designs. Remark~\ref{rem:T1scope} lists the escapes that remain open: a global
  1576	  selector, keys finer than $M^2$, label multiplicity, and relation methods. Smooth-order
  1577	  methods already beat $1/6$ on a positive proportion, but not almost everywhere.
  1578	\item \textbf{Ratios.} See Section~\ref{sec:unbalanced}.
  1579	\item \textbf{Practice.} At 40--80 bits the search is $2.3$--$4.9$ times slower than
  1580	  Harvey--Hittmeir in our proxy. The heuristic subexponential methods (ECM, the quadratic and
  1581	  number field sieves) are far faster still. This work concerns rigorous deterministic bounds
  1582	  only.
  1583	\end{itemize}
