    48	\address{}
    49	\email{}
    50	
    51	\date{DRAFT --- not for distribution. Version of 18 October 2026.}
    52	
    53	\begin{document}
    54	
    55	\begin{abstract}
    56	We give a deterministic algorithm that receives only an integer $N$ and outputs its complete
    57	prime factorisation. On every input its cost is that of the Harvey--Hittmeir algorithm,
    58	$O(N^{1/5}\log^{16/5}N/(\log\log N)^{3/5})$ bit operations, up to lower-order terms. Averaged
    59	over the uniform distribution on the $n$-bit semiprimes $N=pq$ with primes $p<q\le2p$, its
    60	cost is $N^{1/6}(\log N)^{O(1)}$. We know of no previously proved average-case or almost-all
    61	deterministic exponent below $1/5$ for this population.
    62	
    63	The engine is a deterministic weighted collision search, which is a Lehman-type method. It
    64	takes the McKee--Pinch divisibility event $\ell\mid Bp-Aq$ over all primes $\ell\equiv3\pmod
    65	4$ in four dyadic bands near $N^{1/6}$ and all small coprime weights $(A,B)$. It adds the
    66	Harvey--Hittmeir modulus-squared lock, and one baby-step list per band, shared by all primes
    67	of the band through cofactor-powered handles. One run with weight height $H$ costs
    68	$N^{1/6}H^{5/3}(\log N)^{O(1)}$ on every input and never outputs a wrong answer. Its coverage
    69	step misses the true factor on at most an $O((\log N)/H^2)$ and an $O((\log N)^4/H^4)$ fraction
    70	of the pairs; the second bound comes from a two-variable fourth-moment estimate. It gives, for every
    71	$\varepsilon\le1/40$, cost $N^{1/6+5\varepsilon/12+o(1)}$ with exceptional fraction
    72	$O(N^{-\varepsilon})$. The complete algorithm reruns the search with heights
    73	$H\leftarrow H^{6/5}$ behind a baby-step giant-step detector for primes with small
    74	$\ord_q(2)$. This works because the proved failure fraction decays faster in $H$ than the cost
    75	grows.
    76	
    77	We prove two further results. First, the worst case reduces to coverage alone: a modified
    78	search factors every balanced $N$ whose true pair is caught by one band. So a pair-covering
    79	hypothesis implies worst-case cost $N^{1/6+o(1)}$, and any covering theorem with weight
    80	height at most $Y^{1/2}J^{-c}$, for some $c>3/8$, would beat $1/5$ in the worst case. Second, in a sumset
    81	collision-search cost model with residue-local, degree-one targets, reaching the true factor
    82	of a constant fraction of the pairs requires cost $N^{1/6}/(\log N)^{O(1)}$. Our search attains this up to polylogarithmic factors.
    83	(Here costs are per input, or averaged over all $n$-bit integers; budgets averaged only over the
    84	semiprimes are not covered.) The result says nothing about other kinds of
    85	algorithm, and at practical sizes Harvey--Hittmeir is faster.
    86	\end{abstract}
   365	
   366	\paragraph{Hittmeir 2018, 2021.}
   367	Hittmeir \cite{hittmeir2018} combines baby-step/giant-step with sieving of $p+q$ modulo many
   368	small primes and proves $N^{1/4}\exp(-\Omega(\log N/\log\log N))$ [sweep].
   369	Hittmeir~\cite{hittmeir2021} proves $N^{2/9+o(1)}$ through a time--space trade-off for Lehman's
   370	method [sweep]. Hyperbola sieving over many small primes is therefore old. What differs here is
   371	that each prime is used \emph{selectively}: only the at most two classes reached by a short weight
   372	vector are used, rather than all classes being covered.
   373	
   374	\paragraph{Harvey 2021; Harvey--Hittmeir 2022.}
   375	Harvey \cite{harvey2021} proves the deterministic bound $O(N^{1/5}\log^{16/5}N)$, using a single
   376	shared baby-step giant-step search over Lehman's family. He uses the congruences
   377	\[\alpha^{aq+bp}\equiv\alpha^{aN+b}\pmod p\]
   378	and multipoint evaluation over $\Z/N\Z$
   379	(Alg.~4.1), with exact matches handled in Alg.~4.2 [sweep]. Harvey--Hittmeir \cite{hh2022} save a
   380	factor $(\log\log N)^{3/5}$. Their Proposition~3.3 contains the congruence $aq-bp\equiv0\pmod m$
   381	and the modulus-squared lock we use as Lemma~\ref{lem:lock}, with a single modulus and
   382	lattice-chosen weights [sweep]. Harvey remarks that exponent $1/6$ would need a ``fully
   383	square-root speedup'' of Lehman's search [sweep].
   384	
   385	\paragraph{Harvey--Hittmeir 2026; Oznovich--Volk 2025.}
   386	These works give deterministic algorithms that find an element of large multiplicative order
   387	modulo $N$, or a factor \cite{hh2026,oznovichvolk2025} [sweep]. Theorem~\ref{thm:R} adapts
   388	Harvey--Hittmeir's smooth-number subgroup argument and their order dichotomy
   389	(\cite[Lemmas~2.1, 2.3, 2.4 and \S3.1]{hh2026} [sweep]) to a one-sided reading: the first $r$
   390	with $\gcd(b^r-1,N)>1$ gives a factor unless the two orders coincide. Large order modulo $N$ does
   391	not give large order modulo a specific prime. Our average-case theorems therefore never need an
   392	element of large order: $\ord_q(2)$ enters only in counting the exceptional sets ($E_a$, $E_c$,
   393	$C(T)$) and in the cheap detector of Section~\ref{sec:AV}.
   394	
