   233	\begin{remark}[scope]\label{rem:scope}
   234	Each item below is easy to over-read, so we state them plainly.
   235	\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
   236	\item Theorems~\ref{thm:AV} and~\ref{thm:D} concern inputs drawn from $\Sn$, with exceptions
   237	  counted by pairs. Neither gives a worst-case bound below $1/5$. Theorem~\ref{thm:AV} is an
   238	  \emph{expected-time} statement for a complete algorithm; it is not a high-probability
   239	  statement beyond what Theorem~\ref{thm:D} gives.
   240	\item Only ratios $q/p\le2$ are treated. The ratio-band extension is discussed in
   241	  Section~\ref{sec:unbalanced}.
   242	\item ``Never wrong'' means that every output is validated.
   243	\item Theorem~\ref{thm:T1} is a theorem inside a stated cost model. It is not a lower bound for
   244	  factoring. Pollard's $p-1$ method, which lies outside the model, already reaches a positive
   245	  proportion of a nearly balanced family below $N^{1/6}$ (\cite[Prop.~35.3]{wardmaestro2026},
   246	  \cite{friedlander1989}). The cost budget in Theorem~\ref{thm:T1} is per input or averaged over
   247	  all $n$-bit integers; budgets averaged only over $\Sn$ are not covered.
   248	\item All statements are asymptotic. At 40--80 bits the search is $2.3$--$4.9$ times more
