  1104	\begin{remark}
  1105	\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
  1106	\item The ratio $6/5$ is not special. What matters is that the proved tail exponent $2$ in $H$
  1107	  exceeds the cost exponent $5/3$. With a tail $(n/H^2)^j$, any $j>5/6$ suffices after adjusting
  1108	  the schedule. A better tail, such as Theorem~\ref{thm:Eb4}, therefore changes only
  1109	  polylogarithmic factors of the average. (Within these bounds the first rung that fails on
  1110	  fewer than half the pairs already needs $H\gtrsim\sqrt n$, so a factor $n^{c+5/6}$ remains.)
  1111	\item The detector is needed within the proved bounds. Without it the $E_c$ count is limited by
  1112	  primes with $\ord_q(2)\approx\sqrt P$, and the best ladder exponent we can certify is
  1113	  $17/100$ (\texttt{notes/avgtime\_results.md}, \S5.2).
  1114	\item The finish's primality tests and the Harvey--Hittmeir fallback are standard. Only rungs,
  1115	  detector and fallback contribute to the exponent.
  1116	\end{enumerate}
  1117	\end{remark}
